Tensile strength or tensile strength is the strength of a material under tension, i.e., the ability of a material to resist breaking under tension. The term tensile strength is also used in the context of the strength of a fiber or rope, such as a fishing line, a parachute cord, a rope used in climbing or a steel cable used to lift heavy weights.
Tensile strength is a material property, and is not to be confused with tensile strength (ultimate tensile strength) which is the stress at which a material breaks. Tensile strength is an inherent property of a material, and is the maximum strength that the material can achieve, and is not dependent on the size of the material, the dimensions of the test sample, or the test conditions. Tensile strength is also known as ultimate tensile strength.

